# Every language this system knows how to colour, in one place.
#
# ONE PLACE. From this file the generator makes the Rust table the highlighter
# reads, the MIME index that selects a language, the tag index a markdown fence
# uses, and the refusal of an invalid row — all at build time, in the same
# shape as the update's `steps.nix`, `assets/notifications.nix` and
# `assets/tokens/*.nix`.
#
# WHY THIS DECLARES MIME TYPES AND NEVER EXTENSIONS
# --------------------------------------------------
# Because `crates/ronoctua-files/src/mime.rs` already answers "what is this
# file", out of freedesktop's own database: 1 233 name patterns, 496 subclass
# pairs and 323 aliases, generated by `nix/mimegen.nix`. A `"*.rs"` written
# here would be a SECOND place deciding what a file is, and the second place is
# the one that is wrong.
#
# And the inheritance pays. One claim on `application/xml` covers 49
# descendants — `atom+xml`, `gpx+xml`, `mathml+xml`, `xslt+xml` and the rest;
# one on `application/json` covers 8; one on `text/x-python` brings
# `text/x-python3`, `text/x-sagemath` and `text/x-scons`; and the alias table
# hands over `text/x-diff`, `text/x-markdown` and `text/x-yaml` for nothing.
# Thirty-five rows here resolve some hundred and thirty types.
#
# WHAT TO DO WHEN THE DATABASE HAS NO TYPE
# ----------------------------------------
# It happens, and TypeScript is the worked example: `*.ts` is `video/mp2t`
# upstream, and upstream is RIGHT — `.ts` meant an MPEG transport stream years
# before the language existed. Zig, Swift, Vue and Dockerfile simply are not
# there at all.
#
# The answer is NOT a list of extensions in this file. It is one row in the
# `ours` block of `assets/icons/mime-glyphs.nix`, which already exists, already
# carries `text/x-nix`, and already states the bar: upstream must have no type
# at all. A type is invented THERE, with its glob and its icon, and only then
# claimed HERE. The generator refuses a type that is in neither, naming that
# file — so the rule cannot be forgotten, only argued with.
#
# ORDER MATTERS, and there is no `order` field on purpose. `guess` — the door
# for text that arrives with no usable name — tries the languages in the order
# they are written here. The same argument the update's `steps.nix` makes about its
# sequence: two sources for one order is one too many.
#
# WHAT THE GENERATOR REFUSES
# --------------------------
# Seventeen things, each naming the offending `id` and the reason. The short
# version: a type the database does not have; two languages claiming one type;
# a claim on prose or on a container; a language nothing can ever select; a
# `code` row with no words at all; a delimiter over four bytes or not ASCII; a
# comment marker that also opens a string; a block comment with no close; a
# word that is not a word; a word in two of the three lists; a repeated id or
# fence tag; a value outside a closed vocabulary; a field it does not know; a
# `word-chars` that is not ASCII punctuation, or that holds the first
# character of a comment, a string, an attribute or a character literal; a
# `colon-is-constant` with no `:` among the word characters; a `char-prefix`
# that is not a short ASCII marker, or that would hide a comment or a string;
# and a table that came out below its floors.
#
# WHAT A WORD IS, AND THE TWO OPTIONAL FIELDS
# -------------------------------------------
# A word is letters, digits and `_`, `$` and `?` — unless a row says otherwise
# in `word-chars`, the characters besides letters and digits ITS words are
# made of. Lisp is why: `let*`, `multiple-value-bind`, `*standard-output*` and
# `&optional` are one word each, and by the default rule `when-let` lit
# `when`. A row's words are words by its own rule, and the generator checks
# each against it.
#
# `char-prefix` opens a character literal: `#\` in Common Lisp, where `#\"` is
# the quote and `#\;` the semicolon, opening neither a string nor a comment.
# The literal is the prefix, one character, and the word characters after it
# (`#\Space`).
#
# THE CLOSED VOCABULARIES
# -----------------------
#   kind        = code | markup | markdown | diff
#   numbers     = none | plain | c
#   property    = none | colon | equals
#   sniff-shape = none | json-object
#   shapes      subset of { capitalised-is-type, screaming-is-constant,
#                           colon-is-constant }
#
# `colon-is-constant`: a word that starts with `:` is a constant — a Lisp
# keyword (`:test`), never a symbol of a package (`cl:car`).
#
# The Rust matches each of them exhaustively, and a check compares the two
# vocabularies — so a value invented here is a build failure and not a row that
# silently means nothing.
#
# WHAT A LANGUAGE DOES *NOT* DECLARE
# ----------------------------------
# A colour. The highlighter gives back a ROLE and whoever draws turns it into a
# token — the same wall `ronoctua-design` already keeps for layout, and the
# reason a wasm buffer can use this crate without receiving a token value
# (invariant 11).
#
# A text key, either. `label` is a proper noun — "Rust" is Rust in both
# languages — and nothing on screen writes it.

